From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] PackageInstaller: say why a long install stands still

The installing screen shows a progress bar for the copy of the package
into the session and "Installing…" below it. The package manager then
compiles the app with the install filter (speed) before it reports, and
nothing on the screen moves meanwhile: WeRead (13 dex files, 70.5 MB)
takes 1199 s, 20 minutes, on the single Cortex-A9 of the PW3 (Test V40.1).
A user who sees the same screen for minutes is likely to take the device
for hung and restart it in the middle.

Ten seconds after the package manager has the package (after the commit,
or when the screen comes back while the session is still running), the
text below the bar says that the app is being optimized for this device,
that a large one can take 20 minutes or more, that the screen may turn
off meanwhile and that the device must not be turned off or restarted.
Ten seconds is where a wait stops holding attention and should say when
it ends (Nielsen, Usability Engineering, 1993); an install that finishes
sooner never shows it. "Optimizing" is the word Android uses for
compiling apps on the screen of the first boot after an update.
